  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAs of 18z, there are scattered showers over portions of north \ncentral SD. These showers are connected to the Lee of the Rockies \nsurface trough over western SD that has started to move east. This \ntrough will continue to move through western and central SD this \nafternoon and into the evening. A little bit of instability will be \ndeveloping over western SD this afternoon, with models showing \nbetween 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE, and could help some storms to develop \namongst the showers along the boundary. There is even weaker \ninstability shear over central SD that will have a hard time \nsupporting the storms when they move through during the evening into \nthe early overnight hours. This will likely cause the storms to \ndissipate before they are able to reach northeastern SD. With shear \nvalues around 30kts and weak lapse rates, it will be hard for the \nstorms to become severe. However, there could be an occasional non-\nsevere but strong wind gusts from pop up or dying storms. \nAdditionally, with the upper and mid level flow from the southwest, \nthere is a lot of moisture being pulling into central and \nnortheastern SD. This could help the scattered showers and storms to \ndevelop localized heavy downpours this afternoon into the early \novernight hours.\n\nThe showers and storms look to die back overnight into Saturday \nmorning, though some models show an isolated shower or two trying to \npop up occasionally. Coming in behind the surface trough is a cold \nfront that will be moving trough central and northeastern SD \nSaturday morning into the afternoon. With a little bit of \ninstability developing ahead of the front through the day, some \nscattered showers and storms are forecast to develop along it. \nModels show that shear values will be less than 30kt and there will \nbe weak lapse rates, which will help to keep the storms from \nbecoming severe if any develop. The cold front, as well as the \nchances for showers and storms, will be moving out of central and \nnortheastern SD during late Saturday afternoon. Behind the cold \nfront, high surface pressure will be moving in and will help to keep \nprecipitation from forming during the evening overnight into Sunday. \n\nTemperatures will be around normal to slightly cooler than normal \ntoday though Sunday. High temperatures will be in the mid 70s to low \n80s through the weekend. Then a bit of warmer air will be moving in \nSunday night into the beginning of the week, which will help the \ntemperatures to warm slightly. The southwesterly flow aloft will \ncontinue to help to move wildfire smoke out of central and \nnortheastern SD the next couple of days. This will help to keep the \nskies from becoming hazy from the smoke.",
